# Polyglot interop — shared surface.
#
# The polyglot layer bridges Mezze values to guest-language objects via
# the Truffle InteropLibrary. Every guest language ships its own opaque
# `<Lang>Foreign` newtype (see `std::polyglot::jvm::JvmForeign`,
# `std::polyglot::python::PyForeign`) so the compiler prevents
# accidental cross-language mixing. All wrappers share the runtime
# implementation `MezzeForeign`, so per-type tagging is zero-cost at
# runtime.
#
# Each `<Lang>Foreign` type ships an inherent impl block that exposes
# BOTH the language's idiomatic vocabulary (Java: `method`, `new`,
# `static`, `field`, `class_of`, `instanceof`; Python: `method`, `new`,
# `call`, `attr`, `item`, `iter`, `next`, `len`) AND the universal
# InteropLibrary vocabulary (`invoke_member`, `get_member`, `execute`,
# `instantiate`, `array_at`, `array_size`, `as_int`, `as_str`,
# `as_float`, `as_bool`, `is_null`, `type_name`). Every op routes
# through the same underlying `__native_polyglot_*` primitives.
#
# The polyglot Context is managed inside the runtime by the effect
# handler machinery — user code just writes
#   `perform GraalPy in do … let m = Python.import { module = "math" } …`
# and never sees the underlying Truffle Context.
use std::result

## Errors returned by every fallible interop operation.
##
## - NotFound       — the requested member / binding does not exist.
## - TypeError      — the guest value did not accept the requested op.
## - GuestError     — the guest language raised an exception; message
##                    and stack are propagated verbatim.
## - ArityMismatch  — execute/invoke arg count mismatched the target.
## - UnsupportedMessage — the underlying Truffle message is not
##                    supported by the receiver.
pub type InteropError = |
  NotFound { name: Str },
  TypeError { message: Str },
  GuestError { message: Str, guest_stack: Str },
  ArityMismatch { expected: Int, actual: Int },
  UnsupportedMessage,
|
